WEBVTT

00:00:00.105 --> 00:00:01.145
Two words get mixed up.

00:00:01.553 --> 00:00:04.605
A serial schedule runs each transaction as

00:00:04.605 --> 00:00:06.368
one contiguous block.

00:00:06.789 --> 00:00:09.592
A serializable schedule only has to leave

00:00:09.605 --> 00:00:12.132
the database in the same state as some

00:00:12.132 --> 00:00:13.118
serial schedule.

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:14.706
Look at S5.

00:00:15.114 --> 00:00:18.325
Transaction 2's read of x lands in the

00:00:18.338 --> 00:00:20.970
middle of transaction 1's operations, so

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:23.470
this schedule is interleaved and is not

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:24.035
serial.

00:00:24.864 --> 00:00:28.114
Now compare it with S6, a serial schedule,

00:00:28.522 --> 00:00:31.272
where T2's read comes after all of T1.

00:00:31.706 --> 00:00:34.338
S5 has the same reads-from and the same

00:00:34.351 --> 00:00:36.627
final writes as S6.

00:00:37.048 --> 00:00:40.246
So S5 is view-equivalent to a serial

00:00:40.259 --> 00:00:42.706
schedule, and that makes it serializable.

00:00:43.563 --> 00:00:45.484
So interleaving does not break

00:00:45.563 --> 00:00:46.800
serializability.

00:00:47.234 --> 00:00:49.379
What matters is that the outcome matches

00:00:49.392 --> 00:00:50.366
some serial order.

00:00:50.774 --> 00:00:53.800
Now try a new question yourself and ask:

00:00:54.366 --> 00:00:56.405
does the result match a serial schedule?
